= Irradiation
{parent=Physics}
{wiki}

= Radiation
{parent=Physics}

= Ionizing radiation
{parent=Radiation}

Radiation energetic enough to ionize matter. Its biological effects can include direct molecular damage and indirect chemical changes, with consequences depending on dose, timing and cellular context.

= Radiation protection
{parent=Ionizing radiation}

Control of exposure to <ionizing radiation>. Basic physical measures include reducing time near a source, increasing distance and using <radiation shielding>; confinement of radioactive material addresses contamination.

= Radiation dosimetry
{parent=Ionizing radiation}

Measurement or calculation of energy imparted by <ionizing radiation>, together with explicitly specified weighting conventions. <Absorbed dose>, <equivalent dose> and <effective dose> are different quantities.

= Effective dose
{parent=Radiation dosimetry}
{title2=$E=\sum_Tw_TH_T$}

A <tissue weighting factor> weighted sum of <equivalent doses>. Its unit is the <sievert>.

= Tissue weighting factor
{parent=Effective dose}
{title2=$w_T$}

A dimensionless weight in <effective dose>; the weights over the reference tissues sum to one.

= Equivalent dose
{parent=Radiation dosimetry}
{title2=$H_T=\sum_Rw_RD_{T,R}$}

A <radiation weighting factor> weighted sum of mean <absorbed doses> for a specified organ or tissue. Its unit is the <sievert>.

= Radiation weighting factor
{parent=Equivalent dose}
{title2=$w_R$}

A dimensionless factor used to convert a specified radiation component's <absorbed dose> into its contribution to <equivalent dose>.

= Absorbed dose
{parent=Radiation dosimetry}
{title2=$D=d\overline\varepsilon/dm$}

Mean energy imparted by <ionizing radiation> per unit <mass>; its unit is the <gray>.

= Quantum information science
{parent=Physics}

<Quantum information science> studies the storage, processing and transmission of <quantum information>, including the physical implementation of the tasks described by <quantum information theory>.

= Virgo Collaboration
{parent=Physics}
{c}

The scientific collaboration associated with the Virgo gravitational-wave detector.

= LIGO
{parent=Physics}
{c}
{wiki}

Laser Interferometer Gravitational-Wave Observatory: an experimental project operating widely separated laser interferometers for <gravitational-wave> detection.

= LIGO Scientific Collaboration
{parent=LIGO}
{c}
{wiki}

A scientific collaboration, not an individual person.

= Cross section
{disambiguate=physics}
{parent=Physics}
{wiki}
{title2=$\sigma$}

= Cross section
{synonym}

An effective <area> describing the strength of a specified interaction. With an incident <particle flux> $j$ and $N_T$ independent target particles, the ideal event rate for that channel is $jN_T\sigma$.

= Two-body scattering cross section
{parent=Cross section (physics)}
{title2=$\frac{d\sigma}{d\Omega}=\frac1{64\pi^2s}\frac{|\mathbf k_f|}{|\mathbf p_i|}\overline{|\mathcal M|^2}$}

For distinguishable final particles, the <centre-of-momentum frame> cross section is the displayed product of the invariant amplitude, incoming flux and <relativistic two-body phase space>. Initial unpolarized states are averaged; unobserved final polarizations are summed. An isotropic amplitude integrates to $\sigma=(|\mathbf k_f|/|\mathbf p_i|)\overline{|\mathcal M|^2}/(16\pi s)$. A full-solid-angle integral for identical final particles additionally needs its symmetry factor.

= Many-body problem
{parent=Physics}
{wiki}

= Elementary excitation
{parent=Many-body problem}

An approximately independent low-energy excitation above a many-body equilibrium state, characterized by energy, momentum and other quantum numbers. Collective sound and dressed particle-like excitations are examples; the relevant modes depend on the phase and approximation.

= Scintillation
{disambiguate=physics}
{parent=Physics}
{wiki}

= Particle scintillation
{synonym}

A brief flash of <light> produced when an energetic <particle> deposits <energy> in a material. Counting resolved flashes can count individual incident <particles>; this differs from <atmospheric scintillation>.

= Liquid scintillator
{parent=Scintillation (physics)}

A <liquid> that produces <particle scintillation> when deposited <energy> excites its components. <Photomultipliers> can convert the emitted <light> into detector pulses.

= Thought experiment
{parent=Physics}
{wiki}

A <thought experiment> explores the consequences of a specified hypothetical setup using physical or logical assumptions.

= Luminiferous aether
{parent=Physics}
{wiki}

A historical proposed medium for the propagation of <light>. The <Fizeau experiment> tested how moving transparent matter affects light propagation; its positive signal did not uniquely establish such a medium.

= Vacuum
{parent=Physics}
{wiki}

= Physical law
{parent=Physics}

A physical law expresses a reproducible relation used to describe or predict physical phenomena within its domain of applicability. Applying it requires a model specifying the system and conditions: <Newton's second law>, together with a <force> model, predicts a body's <acceleration>; <conservation of energy> constrains exchanges within a specified system. Its predictions must be compared with physical observations.

= Gravity
{parent=Physics}
{wiki}

Gravity governs falling bodies and <orbits>. <Newtonian gravity> describes it through a <gravitational field> acting on masses; <general relativity> instead relates matter and radiation to the geometry of <spacetime> through the <Einstein field equations>.

= Gravitational field
{parent=Gravity}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathbf g$}

In <Newtonian gravity>, the gravitational field $\mathbf g$ specifies the <acceleration> of a freely falling <test particle> at each position. A <gravitational potential> $\Phi$ gives $\mathbf g=-\nabla\Phi$, with the <gradient> taken in the spatial geometry of the model. In <general relativity>, the <metric tensor> describes gravitational effects, and <tidal forces> are encoded by <spacetime> curvature.

= Metrology
{parent=Physics}
{wiki}

<Metrology> asks what a measurement means and how closely it can be reproduced. An instrument reading becomes useful when it is related to a reference through <measurement calibration>, with a stated <measurement uncertainty>. Measuring a changing object against a changing ruler makes the problem especially clear: an observed difference contains the responses of both.

= Measurement accuracy
{parent=Metrology}

<measurement accuracy> concerns closeness to the value of the specified <measurand>; high repeatability alone does not establish it. <calibration>, an adequate response model and assessment of residual systematic effects are required alongside statistical precision.

= Measurand
{parent=Metrology}

The <measurand> is the quantity intended to be measured, specified with enough detail about the system, conditions and averaging convention to distinguish it from instrument output or a different derived quantity.

= International Association for the Properties of Water and Steam
{c}
{parent=Metrology}

= IAPWS
{c}
{synonym}

An organization publishing internationally coordinated formulations and reference data for the physical properties of <water> and steam.

Official website: https://iapws.org

= Work by International Association for the Properties of Water and Steam
{c}
{parent=International Association for the Properties of Water and Steam}

= Dimensional metrology
{parent=Metrology}

Measurement of <length> and geometrical dimensions. A <length standard> supplies a reference; a <differential length comparator> measures the difference between two lengths rather than treating its own structure as perfectly invariant.

= Length standard
{parent=Dimensional metrology}

A reference realizing a specified <length>, under specified conditions. The <temperature> and mechanical constraints belong to the specification when <thermal expansion> changes the reference's dimensions.

= Differential length comparator
{parent=Dimensional metrology}

An instrument for comparing two <lengths> through their difference. Suppose a test rod and reference both have length $L_0$ at <temperature> $T_0$, and have constant <linear thermal expansion coefficients> $\alpha_s$ and $\alpha_r$ over a small interval. Their first-order models give
$$
L_s(T)=L_0[1+\alpha_s(T-T_0)],\qquad L_r(T)=L_0[1+\alpha_r(T-T_0)].
$$
For the observed difference $D(T)=L_s(T)-L_r(T)$, the <derivative> $b=dD/dT$ therefore determines
$$
\boxed{\alpha_s=\alpha_r+\frac{b}{L_0}}.
$$
The reference's independently calibrated <linear thermal expansion coefficient> is essential: the difference alone identifies $\alpha_s-\alpha_r$, not either coefficient separately.

For an original numerical example, stipulate $L_0=1.00\,\mathrm m$, $\alpha_s=1.0\times10^{-6}\,\mathrm{K^{-1}}$ and $\alpha_r=9.0\times10^{-6}\,\mathrm{K^{-1}}$. Heating both by $10\,\mathrm K$ gives sample and reference increases of $10\,\mu\mathrm m$ and $90\,\mu\mathrm m$. Thus **the sample expands even though its measured difference decreases** by $80\,\mu\mathrm m$. These are chosen model parameters, not measured values for a historical <Invar> specimen or reference.

If $\alpha_r$ and $b$ have independent <standard uncertainties> $0.2\times10^{-6}\,\mathrm{K^{-1}}$ and $0.3\,\mu\mathrm{m\,K^{-1}}$, and uncertainty in $L_0$ is negligible, <variance additivity for independent random variables> gives
$$
u(\alpha_s)=\sqrt{u(\alpha_r)^2+\frac{u(b)^2}{L_0^2}}
\simeq0.36\times10^{-6}\,\mathrm{K^{-1}}.
$$
Repeating the difference measurement can reduce its random contribution; it does not remove uncertainty in the reference. A <temperature gradient> or unequal rod <temperatures> also breaks the common-temperature model and can introduce a <systematic error>.

\Image[differential-expansion-and-abba-drift.png]
{title=Chosen rod expansion and instrument drift models: a negative length difference need not imply contraction}

= ABBA measurement sequence
{c}
{parent=Differential length comparator}
{title2=$\widehat{B-A}=(y_1+y_2-y_0-y_3)/2$}

Alternating sample and reference readings can suppress instrument drift. In this original four-reading model, measure objects $A,B,B,A$ at equally spaced times $0,\Delta t,2\Delta t,3\Delta t$. Assume the objects are stable during that short sequence and each reading is its object's <length> plus the same <affine function> of time, $a+bt$. Then
$$
\widehat{B-A}=\frac{y_1+y_2-y_0-y_3}{2}=L_B-L_A,
$$
because both pairs have mean time $3\Delta t/2$. The constant offset and linear drift cancel. With a further drift term $ct^2$, direct substitution instead leaves bias $-2c\Delta t^2$. A shorter sequence reduces that term, but neither this ordering nor averaging corrects unequal <temperatures>, sample changes or reference <measurement uncertainty>. This is a modern illustrative protocol; the historical account below establishes alternation, without attributing this particular four-reading sequence to <Charles-Édouard Guillaume>.

= Guillaume thermal expansion experiment
{c}
{parent=Dimensional metrology}

<Charles-Édouard Guillaume> sought affordable, stable <length standards>. His 1920 lecture describes two micrometer <microscopes> on stone supports sighting marks on a rod immersed in water. Their separation was not fixed: one method alternated the sample with a reference in a second trough at nearly constant <temperature>. His relative method compared sample and <platinum>–<iridium> reference in the same bath, adding the reference's known expansion to the measured difference. Composition and treatment affected the nickel–iron alloys; low expansion held over a limited <temperature> range.<Nobel Lectures, Physics 1901–1921/Invar and Elinvar by Charles-Édouard Guillaume>[Nobel Lectures, Physics 1901–1921; Invar and elinvar, lecture 11 December 1920, pp. 444–448: motivation and comparator; pp. 451–453: composition, treatment and temperature limits]{ref}

\Image[guillaume-expansion-comparator.png]
{title=Functional reconstruction of the water-bath comparison described by <Charles-Édouard Guillaume>, with movable rods and an instrument baseline that can drift}

The <differential length comparator> calculation explains why a calibrated reference is needed even when its expansion is larger than the sample's. Keeping both rods in the same bath targets one source of <systematic error>; allowing for changes in the instrument baseline targets another. Discovering a low-expansion <alloy> and demonstrating its dimensional stability are connected experimental tasks.

= Measurement uncertainty
{parent=Metrology}
{wiki}

A quantified spread of values attributable to a measured quantity, accounting for relevant measurement information and corrections.

= Measurement uncertainty budget
{parent=Measurement uncertainty}

A record of uncertainty contributions in a specified measurement model, their evaluation methods, sensitivity coefficients and relevant <correlations>. For $y=g(x_1,\ldots,x_n)$, first-order <propagation of uncertainty> gives $u_y^2=\sum_{ij}(\partial_i g)(\partial_j g)\operatorname{Cov}(x_i,x_j)$. A correction for a known <systematic error> can itself have uncertainty; applying the correction does not remove that contribution.<Guidelines for Evaluating and Expressing the Uncertainty of NIST Measurement Results by Barry N. Taylor and Chris E. Kuyatt>[sections 4–5 and Appendix A]{ref}

= Standard uncertainty
{parent=Measurement uncertainty}
{title2=$u(x)$}

= Systematic error
{parent=Metrology}
{tag=Measurement error}

= Systematic uncertainty
{parent=Systematic error}

Uncertainty associated with imperfect knowledge of <calibration>, efficiency, backgrounds or other persistent model inputs. Repeating the same measurement does not necessarily average it away. Correlated <nuisance parameters> can propagate it through a <likelihood>; estimating such an uncertainty is distinct from asserting that the systematic error has a known sign and magnitude.

= Common-offset cancellation by signal reversal
{parent=Systematic error}
{title2=$S=(y_+-y_-)/2,\quad b=(y_++y_-)/2$}

If a controlled reversal changes the desired <signal>'s sign while leaving an additive offset unchanged, readings $y_+=S+b$ and $y_-=-S+b$ separate them by half-difference and half-sum. Offset drift, gain changes, hysteresis or a background that also reverses violate the simple cancellation. Interleaving polarities and independent blank measurements test those assumptions; <signal> reversal does not cancel an arbitrary <systematic error>.

= Systematic-error cancellation by reversal
{parent=Systematic error}

If a wanted response changes sign on reversing a controlled setting while an offset remains unchanged, the difference of the reversed readings isolates the response. For $V_\pm=\pm RI+V_0$, the estimator $(V_+-V_-)/(2I)$ cancels $V_0$. The method requires offset stability between readings. It does not cancel a common current-scale <calibration> error, and it does not remove even-in-current <Joule heating>. A useful reversal test specifies which physical effects change sign, then tests the remaining effects independently.

= Measurement calibration
{parent=Metrology}

Comparing an instrument indication with a reference of known value to establish their relation and its <measurement uncertainty>.

= Measurement zero offset
{parent=Measurement calibration}
{title2=$D=S+b$}

A constant additive contribution $b$ to an instrument reading is a zero offset. A blank or zero-signal reading can estimate it, and a <systematic-error cancellation by reversal> can cancel it if it remains unchanged while the desired signal changes sign. The residual correction uncertainty remains part of the <measurement uncertainty>.

= Instrumental drift
{parent=Measurement calibration}

A change in an instrument's response or zero point with time. A reference measured alongside the target can track the component shared by reference and target light.

= Force probe
{parent=Metrology}

A <force probe> measures an applied <force> through a calibrated response. A bending beam with <strain gauges> is one realization; orientation and <measurement calibration> matter.

= Force-restoration balance
{parent=Metrology}

A balance that applies a compensating <force> to hold its load support near a reference position; the compensation <signal> measures the applied <force>.

