= Scientific instrument
{parent=Experimental apparatus}
{wiki}

= Load cell
{parent=Scientific instrument}
{wiki}

A <load cell> is a calibrated device for measuring <force>. In a <tensile modulus experiment> its output supplies the axial load used to calculate <engineering stress>.

= Telescope
{parent=Scientific instrument}
{wiki}

= Chandra X-ray Observatory
{parent=Telescope}
{c}

An <X-ray> observatory used for high-resolution imaging and spectroscopy of astronomical sources. Its observations of the <Perseus cluster> reveal structure in the emitting intracluster <gas> around radio bubbles.<A deep Chandra observation of the Perseus cluster: shocks and ripples by A. C. Fabian and others>[2003, abstract]{ref}

= Kepler space telescope
{c}
{parent=Telescope}

= Kepler satellite
{c}
{synonym}

A space observatory that measured stellar <light curves> to discover <exoplanets> and constrain their <exoplanet occurrence rate> through <exoplanet transit photometry>.

= Faraday cup
{c}
{parent=Scientific instrument}
{wiki}

A collector that measures incident charged-particle <electric charge>.

= Oscillograph
{parent=Scientific instrument}
{wiki}

An instrument that records a time-varying electrical <signal>.

= Microscope
{parent=Scientific instrument}
{wiki}

= Dounce homogenizer
{parent=Scientific instrument}
{c}

= Cathetometer
{parent=Scientific instrument}
{wiki}

= Photogate
{parent=Scientific instrument}

A <photogate> detects interruption of a light beam. Timing that interruption and independently measuring the flag length gives its average <speed> during passage; the direction must be assigned separately.

= Ionization chamber
{parent=Scientific instrument}

= Proportional counter
{parent=Scientific instrument}
{wiki}

A gas-filled <particle detector> using <electron avalanche> multiplication. Within its proportional operating range, the collected <electric charge> tracks the initial <ionization>, allowing more than a yes-or-no count.

= Potter-Elvehjem homogenizer
{c}
{parent=Scientific instrument}

A <Potter-Elvehjem homogenizer> mechanically disrupts <biological tissue> between a pestle and a surrounding tube.

= Capillary tube
{parent=Experimental apparatus}

= History of physics
{parent=Physics}
{wiki}

A discovery becomes more intelligible when its motivating question, proposed explanation and actual measurement can be followed together. The <photoelectric effect> connects <Albert Einstein>'s light-quantum reasoning with the <Millikan photoelectric experiment>. The <Schrödinger equation> and the <Davisson–Germer experiment> connect mathematical <wave mechanics> with a laboratory test of <matter waves>.

The <Nobel Prize in Physics> provides one route through these subjects. Following the original papers and the people who built the apparatus also brings collaborators into view: <Lester Halbert Germer> appears alongside <Clinton J. Davisson> in the <Davisson–Germer experiment>. The history belongs in the articles on those people and concepts as well as in award records.

